<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kaga Ikko-ikki</h1><p class="st-meta">1488 CE – 1580 CE · capital Oyama Gobo (Kanazawa)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A province of Japan ruled for almost a century by leagues of Jodo Shinshu Buddhist believers after they overthrew its governor in 1488.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The preaching of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennyo">Rennyo</a> spread the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">True Pure Land</a>, or Ikko, sect among farmers, local warriors and townspeople of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokuriku-region">Hokuriku region</a>. In 1488 a league of believers rose against the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togashi-masachika">Togashi Masachika</a>, besieged his castle and drove him to suicide.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Togashi relative remained nominal governor, but real power lay with leagues of local warriors and village communities organised around temples of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ishiyama-hongan-ji">Hongan-ji</a>, whose head priests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamashina-hongan-ji">Yamashina</a> and later Ishiyama directed affairs. In 1546 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyama-gobo">Oyama Gobo</a> temple was founded at the site of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanazawa">Kanazawa</a> as the regional centre. Contemporaries called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaga-province">Kaga</a> a province held by peasants.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikko leagues</a> for years. After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ishiyama-hongan-ji">Ishiyama Hongan-ji</a> surrendered in 1580, his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibata-katsuie">Shibata Katsuie</a> captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyama-gobo">Oyama Gobo</a> and crushed resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaga-province">Kaga</a> later became the domain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeda-clan">Maeda</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kaga-ikko-ikki.jpg" alt="Kaga Ikko-ikki" width="320" height="335"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Kaga.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1488 CE – 1580 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Oyama Gobo (Kanazawa)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Jodo Shinshu Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Japan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kaga ikki, Ikko-ikki of Kaga, Peasants' Kingdom of Kaga</dd></dl></aside></div>

